Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Convert markdown to HTML using regex
    std::regex regex(R"(
        (\`.*?\\```)(<p>(.*?)</p>)
    )");
    std::string output;
    std::string match;
    while (std::regex_replace(markdown, match, regex)) {
        output += match[1];
    }
    return output;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "```cpp\nvoid main() {n    std::cout << \"Hello, World!\" <<\\n}}\n```";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses regular expressions to convert markdown code blocks (enclosed in triple backticks) to HTML paragraphs (enclosed in `<p>` tags). The `std::regex_replace` function is used to replace the markdown patterns with the corresponding HTML patterns. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function to convert a simple markdown string to HTML.

